Yes,  cons is actually a binary operator that produces a result.
(See  Section 6.3.6 in  the manual) NIL is a constructor that creates an
empty list of a given type.

eg.


GNODE var;
GNODE alist;
/* Suppose DealWithArg returns a node of type Expr */

(ASSIGN alist (NIL  Expression))  /* Create an empty list of Expressions */
<callargs
   {Expression
            (ASSIGN alist (CONS (CALL DealWithArg) alist))
   }
>

/* As long as DealWithArg always returns a GNODE */

/* Now you can do something with the list you just constructed, like
   this  (Page 11 of manual ) */

(TRAVERSE  Expression alist {Expression  (?etc etc )} )





